 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DebBWc5lGGU/",
 "title": "CIA Tactic to Get Information Without Asking Questions",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains elicitation, a CIA tactic used to get information out of people without asking direct questions. The main lesson is that people naturally correct false statements or share their own experiences when provoked by a statement, making it more effective than direct interrogation.",
 "key_points": [
  "Use statements instead of asking pointed questions so people do not feel pressed or interrogated.",
  "Triggering the need to correct the record: Make a bold, incorrect claim to make the person correct you with the actual facts (e.g., claiming Whole Foods employees make $26/hour to get someone making $17/hour to correct you).",
  "Share an observation statement: Follow up by saying something like 'I bet you had some interesting experiences doing that' or 'I can imagine that was challenging' to get someone to talk more.",
  "Use disbelief: Express mild shock or disbelief about a topic (like a flawless trip or job) to prompt the other person to defend or explain the reality, leading them to volunteer information without you asking a single question.",
  "Elicitation works because the brain does not set off security alarms when it is not being directly questioned."
 ],
 "content": "Comment “FREE” and Chase’s free human behavior training will be sent to you. Chase Hughes teaches human behavior, influence, and behavioral profiling built from two decades of military and intelligence experience. Follow @chasehughesclips for more. I'll give you a an example. Let's say you and I walk into Whole Foods. Let's say there's a young lady stocking produce and you get in there and I say, your goal is to go figure out how much she makes in 60 seconds and you're not allowed to ask any questions. So if if you went over to her, and this is a a generalization just to get you to understand what elicitation is, and said, hey, I'm trying to find the baby carrots. And she walks you over there, but while you're walking, you say, I just read this article online that says all Whole Foods employees just got bumped up to $26/hr, that's fantastic. And she turns around and goes, what? No, I I make $17. So now she doesn't feel like she's been pressed or questioned about how much she makes, she's correcting you. Or if you let's say you got into an Uber tonight and you said, I just read this article that Uber drivers were one of the top most highly satisfied employees in the country, you're gonna get a correction and you're gonna be like, what? Where did you read that? So triggering a need to correct the record is one of the easiest ways to use elicitation, but it's only one. Another one is just making a statement afterwards and saying, I bet you had some interesting experiences doing that, or I can imagine that was challenging. Statements are always going to be better than asking pointed questions because a person feels like they're volunteering information. The third layer of that is disbelief. So let's say I wanted to ask if you just got back from vacation and I didn't want to use any questions. We let's say we meet up for dinner or something and I said, Steven, you look like you just got back from a vacation. That's a statement and you're like, no, actually, I've been doing this, this, this and this. So now you've you've given me more than if I just asked if you were on vacation and I said, wow, that had to be interesting. I I can't imagine that's got to be a lot of stuff going on there and you start talking more and more and more. And then like that sounds great, there was no challenges to that entire trip. I love when everything is 100% flawless. And you're like, no, actually, and then you start going into that and I'm like, no way, now we have disbelief that comes in and I haven't asked a single question yet. So that's one thing that I challenge everybody that I train to get really good at is that elicitation piece. How many layers can I get into a person or a conversation just using these statements? But that elicitation is powerful because we're not being asked questions, so our brain doesn't set off little security alarms.",
